export const SEO_WRITING_SKILL = "---\nname: seo-writing\ndescription: SEO writing for pages that need to rank on Google and get cited in AI answers. Use when Jacob asks to write or rewrite a blog post, guide, service page or location page for a target keyword, or to build out a set of pages from one pattern. Also writes the coding-agent handoff when the page will be built. Other skills reach for it for length, metadata and linking rules.\n---\n\n# SEO writing\n\nEvery run produces one **copy doc**: the page's words, metadata, link plan and a check report. The copy doc says what the page contains. Layout, hero, buttons, images, fonts and colors belong to the separate UI agent, so the copy doc stays silent on them.\n\n## Sources of truth\n\n| Source | Owns | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| [PARAMETERS.md](PARAMETERS.md) | Every number and rule: content requirements, length, keywords, metadata, links, overlap, SERP exclusions | Steps 2 to 5 and 8. Change the skill's behavior here. |\n| copywriting skill | Persuasion: clarity, benefits, customer language, headline and CTA options | Step 6 |\n| anti-ai-writing skill | Voice: banned vocabulary and core rules | Step 7 |\n| seo-audit skill | Its \"Content Quality Assessment\" section (E-E-A-T) | Steps 3 and 8. PARAMETERS.md replaces its numeric limits. |\n| programmatic-seo skill | Batch branch | [BATCH.md](BATCH.md) |\n| [HANDOFF.md](HANDOFF.md) | The coding-agent handoff | Step 9, when a coding agent will build the page |\n| [scripts/serp.py](scripts/serp.py) | Measures the top 3 results | Step 3 |\n| [scripts/check.py](scripts/check.py) | Mechanical checks on the draft | Step 8. The draft is **green** when the report shows zero FAIL. |\n\nWhen sources disagree, this order wins: confirmed facts, then PARAMETERS.md, then anti-ai-writing, then copywriting.\n\n## Branches\n\n- **New page** (default): steps 1 to 9.\n- **Rewrite**: keep the URL. Keep the primary keyword unless the search data says it is wrong, and flag it if so. Step 1 adds the current page text and the queries it ranks for. The copy doc lists what changed and why.\n- **Batch** (three or more pages from one pattern, such as \"[service] in [city]\"): read [BATCH.md](BATCH.md) before step 1.\n\n## Step 1: Pick the search\n\nDecide the one search this page exists to win: the words a real customer types when they need what the page offers.\n\n- **Search**: the query, from Search Console data, Jacob's notes, or the obvious customer phrasing of the service and place.\n- **Intent**: informational (learning), commercial (comparing), transactional (ready to hire) or local (near me, in a place). The intent decides the page type.\n- **Page type**: service, location or blog, plus the subtype from the page types table in PARAMETERS.md (neighborhood, city, specific job, brand, hub, guide). The subtype decides what the page owns.\n- **SERP fit**: search the query and look at what Google ranks. If page one is mostly aggregators, directories or forums, or the ranking pages are a different type than planned, stop and tell Jacob before writing; the search may be the wrong one.\n- **Ownership**: pull the sitemap and list every page on the site that targets the same search. If one already owns it, stop and tell Jacob; the job may be a rewrite. Otherwise write the ownership line: the one question this page answers that no other page on the site answers.\n\nDone when the search, intent, page type, SERP fit and ownership line are written at the top of the copy doc.\n\n## Step 2: Choose the keywords\n\n- **Primary keyword**: the search from step 1, in the form people type it. Its placement, spread and density follow the `keywords` rules in PARAMETERS.md.\n- **Variants**: two to four synonyms or close variants of the primary, so the page covers the topic without repeating one phrase.\n- **Secondary keywords**: two to five related searches that belong on the same page. Pull them from SearchAtlas `se_lookup_keyword` (volume, difficulty, intent and related keywords; pass a `location_id` for city searches), Google's People Also Ask, related searches, autocomplete and Search Console. A secondary keyword with its own distinct intent belongs on its own page; list it for Jacob instead.\n- Add impressions and position for each from Search Console when Jacob has them. This is the 30-day baseline.\n\nDone when the primary, every variant and every secondary keyword are written with their source, and each secondary is confirmed to share the primary's intent.\n\n## Step 3: Study the top 3\n\nThe goal is a plan to **dethrone** the three pages Google ranks highest for the primary keyword.\n\n1. Pull the SERP from the most faithful source available:\n   - **Local intent**: run the Google search in Claude in Chrome with the place in the query, since local results shift by location. Without Chrome, use the SearchAtlas SERP overview with the place in the keyword.\n   - **Everything else**: SearchAtlas SERP overview (`se_research_keywords` to save the keyword, then `se_keyword_research_projects` with `mode=serp`).\n   - **Fallback**: web search, noted in the copy doc as the source.\n\n   Take the top three organic results, skipping every domain in `serp_exclude` in PARAMETERS.md (Reddit, social media, Yelp, Trustpilot and other aggregators) and any result that is a different page type.\n2. Run `python3 scripts/serp.py URL1 URL2 URL3`. It measures each page against the content requirements and prints the length target. When it skips a page it can't fetch, open that page in Claude in Chrome, read the text with `get_page_text`, count words, H2s and lists by hand, and add the row. Without Chrome, take the next organic result instead. Either way, the target rests on three pages.\n3. Read each page in full and write a **teardown** for each: what it covers (its H2 subtopics), what it gets wrong or leaves out, how specific it is (numbers, named parts, places, prices, proof), its freshness, and its E-E-A-T signals from seo-audit's \"Content Quality Assessment\" section.\n4. Write the **dethrone plan**:\n   - every subtopic two or more of the three cover (the price of entry)\n   - one to three subtopics none of them cover well, drawn from confirmed facts (the reason to rank this page over them)\n   - where this page will beat them on specifics and proof\n   - secondary keywords the competitors' headings surfaced, added to step 2's list if they share its intent\n   - the length target from serp.py\n\nDone when all three teardowns are written, the dethrone plan holds every two-of-three subtopic and at least one subtopic the three miss, and the length target is a written number.\n\n## Step 4: Gather facts and links\n\n- **Confirmed facts**, each with its source (client brief, About page, owner's written answers, job notes, reviews). Anything without a source gets an inline `[CONFIRM: question for the owner]`.\n- **Voice of customer**: real wording from reviews, job notes and customer questions.\n- **Conversion action**: the one thing a reader should do.\n- **Internal link targets** from the sitemap, per the page type's required targets in PARAMETERS.md, each confirmed to return 200.\n- **External sources** for the claims that need support, in the source order in PARAMETERS.md.\n\nClaim rules:\n\n- **Accuracy beats selling.** If work is sent out (a rebuild going to an outside builder, a part machined elsewhere), say so plainly. Never imply in-house work that isn't.\n- **Written sign-off first** for prices, warranties, turnaround times, certifications not on the About page, offers without an end date, and services outside the confirmed list. Without it, the claim stays out or carries a `[CONFIRM]` tag.\n- **Current names only**: the client's retired brand names never appear.\n\nDone when every field is filled or carries a `[CONFIRM]` tag and the link targets meet the content requirements.\n\n## Step 5: Outline\n\nWrite the H2 and H3 skeleton from the dethrone plan. Give each heading a word budget, the keyword it carries, and its link slots, written as `[int: /url/]` and `[ext: url, claim it supports]`. Mark where each list goes. Phrase a heading as a question when the subtopic is one people ask.\n\nDone when every subtopic in the dethrone plan has a heading, every secondary keyword and variant has a home, the primary has a spot in each third of the page, the budgets sum to the length target within the tolerance in PARAMETERS.md, and the outline's H2, list and link counts sit inside the content requirements.\n\n## Step 6: Draft\n\nLoad the **copywriting** skill and answer its \"Before Writing\" questions from steps 1 to 4. On top of it:\n\n- The H1 leads with the primary keyword and reads as a headline a person would write. Copywriting's headline formulas go in the subhead and H2s.\n- **Answer first**: the first one or two sentences under each heading answer that heading outright. AI answers lift these sentences.\n- Every section carries at least one specific: a number, a named place, part, standard or step, drawn from confirmed facts.\n- Lists hold content that is genuinely a list: steps, parts, symptoms, what's included. Write list items as plain sentences or phrases (anti-ai-writing rules out bold-header bullets).\n- Reach the length target with **useful content**, never restatement. When the draft runs short, add sections in this order:\n  1. People Also Ask questions none of the three answer well\n  2. Subtopics only one of the three covers\n  3. Depth a customer would want before buying: what the work involves, what drives cost, how long it takes, what to expect, how to choose\n  4. Owner questions that would unlock a section, tagged `[CONFIRM]` with the section drafted around the answer\n- Put each link on the words that describe the page it points to.\n- Give two or three options for the title, subhead and main CTA sentence, each with a one-line reason, and mark the recommended one.\n\nWrite the page copy in the draft format check.py reads:\n\n```markdown\n---\nprimary: brake repair norterra\nvariants: [brake service in norterra, norterra brake shop]\nsecondary: [brake pad replacement, squealing brakes, brake inspection cost]\ntype: location\nsite: example.com\nslug: /norterra-brake-repair/\ntitle: Brake repair in Norterra | Example Auto\nmeta: 140 to 155 characters of checkable facts with the primary keyword in them.\nwords_target: 1100\ncompetitors: [rivalshop.com]\n---\n\n# Brake repair in Norterra\n\nBody copy with [descriptive anchor](/service/brakes/) links...\n```\n\nDone when every outline slot holds copy and every link slot holds a link.\n\n## Step 7: Edit\n\nLoad the **anti-ai-writing** skill and run every word through it: title, meta, headings, body, lists, FAQ and anchors. Work its if/then checks paragraph by paragraph. Then recheck the step 6 options, since persuasive lines are where AI tells return.\n\nDone when every paragraph has passed. Note the notable fixes in the copy doc.\n\n## Step 8: Check\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/check.py draft.md --live --siblings <sibling URLs or files>\n```\n\nFix every FAIL and rerun until the report is green. Read each WARN and either fix it or note why it stands. Then mark each E-E-A-T signal from seo-audit's \"Content Quality Assessment\" section as present on the page or tagged `[CONFIRM]`, and confirm the page beats each teardown on the points the dethrone plan named.\n\nDone when the report is green and pasted into the copy doc, every E-E-A-T signal is marked, and every dethrone point is met or flagged.\n\n## Step 9: Deliver\n\nSave a new file named `[client]-[slug]-copy.md` in the client's folder. Jacob's existing notes stay untouched unless he asks for an edit. The copy doc holds, in order:\n\n1. Search, intent, page type and ownership line (step 1)\n2. Keywords with sources and baseline (step 2)\n3. Teardowns, serp.py table and dethrone plan (step 3)\n4. Metadata options (step 6)\n5. Page copy in draft format\n6. **Link plan**: internal links out; external links, each with the claim it supports and a short line from the source that supports it; and an **inbound edits** table of existing pages to change, each row with the page, the exact sentence and the anchor to wrap\n7. **Schema notes**: `BlogPosting` for blog, `Service` for service and location, `BreadcrumbList` matching the real hierarchy, and `FAQPage` when the page has an FAQ, with text identical to the visible copy\n8. Anti-ai-writing fixes and the check report\n\nWhen a coding agent will build the page, also write `[client]-[slug]-handoff.md` per [HANDOFF.md](HANDOFF.md).\n\nReply to Jacob in two or three lines: what was written, the `[CONFIRM]` list, and any decision he owes (slug, title option, an overlap problem, a page that came in short, a secondary keyword that wants its own page). If the page counts toward a contract quota (for example \"4 pages a month, at least 2 new\"), say whether it counts as new or as a rewrite.\n";

export const SEO_WRITING_PARAMETERS = "# SEO writing parameters\n\nThe one place to change how seo-writing behaves. scripts/check.py and scripts/serp.py read the JSON block below, so a number changed here changes the check too. The prose explains each rule by its key.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"content\": {\n    \"words\": [900, 2500],\n    \"h1\": 1,\n    \"h2\": [2, 20],\n    \"paragraphs\": [5, 30],\n    \"external_links\": [1, 3],\n    \"lists\": [1, 4]\n  },\n  \"types\": {\n    \"service\":  {\"faq\": [4, 6]},\n    \"location\": {\"faq\": [4, 6]},\n    \"blog\":     {\"faq\": [0, 5]}\n  },\n  \"length\": {\"parity_ceiling\": 1.3, \"tolerance\": 0.15},\n  \"keywords\": {\"secondary\": [2, 5], \"variants\": [2, 4], \"primary_density\": [0.003, 0.015], \"primary_density_max\": 0.02, \"primary_h2_share_max\": 0.5},\n  \"metadata\": {\"title_max\": 60, \"meta\": [140, 155]},\n  \"internal\": {\"per_1000_words\": [2, 5], \"min\": 2, \"blog_min\": 3, \"inbound_min\": 3, \"anchor_words\": [2, 7]},\n  \"readability\": {\"paragraph_max_sentences\": 4, \"max_words_between_headings\": 350},\n  \"overlap\": {\"target\": 0.25, \"watch\": 0.30, \"rewrite\": 0.45},\n  \"serp_exclude\": [\"reddit.com\", \"quora.com\", \"facebook.com\", \"instagram.com\", \"tiktok.com\", \"youtube.com\", \"x.com\", \"twitter.com\", \"linkedin.com\", \"pinterest.com\", \"nextdoor.com\", \"yelp.com\", \"trustpilot.com\", \"angi.com\", \"angieslist.com\", \"homeadvisor.com\", \"thumbtack.com\", \"bbb.org\", \"yellowpages.com\", \"mapquest.com\", \"houzz.com\", \"porch.com\", \"google.com\", \"wikipedia.org\", \"indeed.com\", \"glassdoor.com\"],\n  \"generic_anchors\": [\"click here\", \"here\", \"learn more\", \"read more\", \"more\", \"this page\", \"this article\", \"this post\", \"link\", \"website\"]\n}\n```\n\n## Page types\n\n`types` in the JSON holds the three types check.py knows. Each has subtypes, and the subtype decides the one question the page owns, so sibling pages never compete for the same search.\n\n| Type | Subtype | Owns |\n|---|---|---|\n| location | Neighborhood (Norterra brake repair) | Proximity and local driving: the actual roads, how close it is, local conditions. \"Is this shop near me.\" |\n| location | City (Phoenix brake repair) | Reach: freeway access, coverage across the metro. \"Is this shop worth the drive.\" |\n| service | Specific job (Duramax repair) | Depth on one job the owner does well: generations, known failures, how it's diagnosed |\n| service | Brand (GM repair) | The owner's background with that make, the models that come in, a real job as proof |\n| service | Hub (/service/brakes/) | The whole service line across all areas. No place in the title or H1. |\n| blog | Guide | One question answered (symptoms, cost factors, how to choose), linking to the service page that converts |\n\nEvery location page carries a **local cause** section: why this problem happens here (heat, commute pattern, terrain, the vehicles common in the area). It is the section that separates the page from its siblings.\n\n## Content requirements\n\nHard limits for every page, checked by check.py. Count only the body copy under the H1: navigation, footer, breadcrumbs and sitewide components don't count.\n\n| Element | Key | What counts |\n|---|---|---|\n| Words | `content.words` | Words under the H1, headings and lists included |\n| H1 | `content.h1` | Exactly one |\n| H2 | `content.h2` | Every H2, FAQ heading included |\n| Paragraphs | `content.paragraphs` | Prose blocks, FAQ answers included; headings, lists and tables excluded |\n| Internal links | `internal.per_1000_words`, never under `internal.min` (`internal.blog_min` on blogs) | Body links to the same site |\n| External links | `content.external_links` | Body links to other sites |\n| Lists | `content.lists` | Each bulleted or numbered list counts once, however many items |\n\n## Length\n\nInside the `content.words` limits, the top 3 set the target. Google ranks pages that satisfy the query, and the pages already ranking show how much it takes.\n\n- **Target** = the median main-content word count of the three pages from step 3 (serp.py prints it), raised to the `content.words` floor or lowered to its ceiling when the median falls outside.\n- **Ceiling over parity**: no more than the median times `length.parity_ceiling`. Extra words go to subtopics the three miss, never to restatement.\n- The draft passes when it lands within `length.tolerance` of the target.\n- FAQ pairs per page: `types.*.faq`. Each answer opens with a sentence that answers the question.\n\n## Keywords\n\nOne primary keyword per page, used **consistently**: in every required spot, spread across the whole body, and never stuffed.\n\n- **Required spots**: H1 (leading it where the sentence allows), title (first half), slug, meta description and the first paragraph.\n- **Spread**: the primary appears in each third of the body (opening, middle, closing), so it runs through the page instead of clustering at the top.\n- **Density**: primary mentions divided by body words lands within `keywords.primary_density` (0.3% to 1.5%, about 3 to 15 mentions per 1,000 words). Over `keywords.primary_density_max` (2%) reads stuffed and fails. A mention counts with small words in between, so \"brake repair in Norterra\" counts for \"brake repair norterra\".\n- **Headings**: the primary may carry H2s, but no more than `keywords.primary_h2_share_max` of them. The rest of the headings use secondaries, variants or plain language.\n- **Variants**: `keywords.variants` synonyms or close variants of the primary (\"brake service in Norterra\", \"brake work\", \"Norterra brake shop\"), each used at least once. Variants carry the topic where the exact phrase would repeat.\n- **Secondary keywords**: `keywords.secondary` per page, each sharing the primary's intent. Each appears in the body at least once, and at least one carries an H2. A secondary that makes a heading awkward moves to the body.\n\n## Metadata and on-page\n\n- **Title**: at most `metadata.title_max` characters, primary keyword in the first half, the site's brand suffix at the end.\n- **Meta description**: within `metadata.meta` characters, built from checkable facts (credential, place, what's covered) with the primary keyword in it.\n- **Headings**: sentence case, no skipped levels (H2 then H3, never H2 then H4).\n- **Slug**: short, lowercase, hyphenated, built from the primary keyword's words, in the site's pattern.\n- **Readability**: paragraphs of at most `readability.paragraph_max_sentences` sentences, and a heading at least every `readability.max_words_between_headings` words.\n\n## Internal links\n\nCount only links in the body copy. Navigation, footer, breadcrumbs and sitewide CTA components don't count.\n\n- **Count**: `internal.per_1000_words` (about 2 to 4 on a 900-word page, 5 to 12 on a 2,500-word page), never under `internal.min`, and never under `internal.blog_min` on a blog post.\n- **Required targets by type**, which fit inside that count:\n  - service: its parent hub, one sibling service, the conversion page\n  - location: the service hub, one nearby location page, the conversion page\n  - blog: the money page it supports (linked in the first third of the post) and one related post\n- **One URL, one link**: each URL is linked once in the body, where it is most relevant.\n- **One anchor, one URL**: the same anchor text never points at two different pages.\n- **Anchors** run `internal.anchor_words` words and describe the page they point to (\"brake repair in Norterra\"). Vary them; a sibling's exact target query appears as an anchor at most once per page. The `generic_anchors` list never appears as anchor text.\n- **Clean targets**: link only to URLs that return 200, are indexable and are their own canonical. Point at the final URL, not a redirect.\n- **Inbound**: the link plan names at least `internal.inbound_min` existing pages to edit, each with the exact sentence and the anchor to wrap. A page nothing links to is an orphan.\n\n## External links\n\nExternal links back up facts. Each one sits on a specific claim, and its anchor names the fact or the source (\"NHTSA's 2024 recall data\").\n\n- **Count**: `content.external_links`. Every page links out at least once, so every page carries at least one claim worth a source.\n- **Source order**, best first:\n  1. Government and regulators (.gov, state agencies, NHTSA, EPA, OSHA, building codes)\n  2. Standards bodies and manufacturer documentation (ASE, ANSI, OEM service bulletins and specs)\n  3. Original research and peer-reviewed studies\n  4. Recognized trade associations\n  5. Major publications reporting on 1 to 4\n- **Dates**: write the year into any sentence with a statistic. Prefer sources from the last three years unless the fact is stable (a code, a spec).\n- **Verified**: the page returns 200 and states the claim. The link plan quotes the supporting line, under 15 words.\n- **Who gets linked**: sources that hold one of the five ranks above. Competitors in the client's market, other agencies, affiliate listicles and content farms stay out (list the client's competitors under `competitors` in the draft so check.py flags them).\n- **rel**: followed by default. `rel=\"sponsored\"` for anything paid, `rel=\"ugc\"` for forums and user content. If a source only feels safe with nofollow, find a better source.\n\n## Overlap\n\n6-gram Jaccard overlap between the draft and each sibling page's live text (check.py `--siblings`).\n\n- Under `overlap.target`: good.\n- Over `overlap.watch`: rewrite the shared passages if they're in the body.\n- Over `overlap.rewrite`: the page fails and gets rewritten.\n";

export const ANTI_AI_WRITING_SKILL = "---\nname: anti-ai-writing\ndescription: Write and rewrite prose so it reads as human, not AI-generated — strips AI tells (puffery, significance inflation, rule-of-three stacking, vague attribution, em dashes, -ing impact clauses). Use when writing or editing any user-facing copy: website content, landing pages, blog posts, articles, marketing copy, About pages, press releases, or any prose that must not sound AI-written. Other writing skills should reach for this to enforce human voice.\n---\n\n# Anti-AI Writing\n\nRules for prose that doesn't read as AI-generated. Apply all of them to any prose you write or edit. Rules only, no justification unless it disambiguates.\n\n## Banned vocabulary — never use these\n\n**Puffery / significance inflation:**\ndelve, tapestry, vibrant, rich (figurative), profound, groundbreaking (figurative), renowned, nestled, in the heart of, boasts, showcasing, exemplifies, commitment to, natural beauty\n\n**Abstract filler:**\ncrucial, pivotal, vital, key (adjective), valuable, intricate/intricacies, interplay, enduring, garner, align with, resonate with, encompasses, cultivating, fostering (figurative)\n\n**Weak verb substitutes:**\nunderscore (as verb), highlight (as verb), emphasize (as verb), reflect (figurative), symbolize, contribute to, enhance\n\n**Transition clichés:**\nAdditionally (sentence-opener), Furthermore, Moreover, It is worth noting, It is important to note\n\n**Sycophantic openers:**\nCertainly!, Of course!, Great question!, Absolutely!, I hope this helps, Let me know if you need anything else\n\n**Legacy / impact phrases:**\nstands as a testament, marks a pivotal moment, setting the stage for, evolving landscape, focal point, indelible mark, deeply rooted, lasting legacy, broader trends, represents a shift\n\n## Core rules\n\n1. **Use \"is/are\" over elaborate substitutes.** BAD: \"serves as,\" \"stands as,\" \"marks,\" \"represents,\" \"features,\" \"boasts\". GOOD: \"is,\" \"has,\" \"was\".\n2. **Never inflate significance.** If a fact is mundane, say it plainly. Do not explain why it matters unless the source says so.\n3. **No vague attribution.** BAD: \"Experts argue,\" \"Observers note,\" \"Several sources suggest,\" \"Critics have described\". GOOD: name the source, or drop the claim.\n4. **No superficial analysis appended via -ing phrases.** BAD: \"The report was released, highlighting its importance for regional development.\" GOOD: \"The report was released.\" (If the importance is sourced, state it as a separate sentence with attribution.)\n5. **No rule-of-three stacking unless the list is genuinely exhaustive and meaningful.** BAD: \"dynamic, innovative, and forward-thinking approach\". GOOD: pick one specific word or none.\n6. **No \"not only X but Y\" parallelisms.** BAD: \"Not just a building, it's a symbol of community.\" GOOD: \"It is a community center.\"\n7. **No false ranges.** BAD: \"from technology to tradition,\" \"from science to storytelling\". GOOD: list items directly, or omit.\n8. **No elegant variation to dodge repetition.** Repeat the noun. Do not substitute: protagonist, key player, eponymous figure, the aforementioned, said [noun].\n9. **No \"Despite its [positive], [subject] faces challenges\" formula.**\n10. **No title case in section headings.** Sentence case only.\n11. **No emoji in professional writing.**\n12. **Never use the em dash (—).** Use a comma, parenthesis, colon, or period instead.\n13. **No bullet + bold-header lists** (e.g., **Term**: description) unless a glossary is explicitly requested.\n14. **No promotional language about companies, places, or people** not sourced to a promotional context.\n\n## If/then decision logic\n\n- **IF** about to write \"serves as\" or \"stands as\" → write \"is\".\n- **IF** about to write \"Additionally\" at the start of a sentence → delete it, or restructure to connect ideas directly.\n- **IF** about to explain why something is significant → ask: does the source say this? If no, cut it.\n- **IF** a sentence ends in an -ing clause that explains impact → cut the clause, keep the fact.\n- **IF** stacking three adjectives → pick one or rewrite as a concrete detail.\n- **IF** attributing to \"experts,\" \"observers,\" or \"critics\" with no name → name the source or remove the claim.\n- **IF** describing challenges at the end of a piece → check for the formula [positive] → [faces challenges] → [optimistic close]. If present, break it.\n- **IF** the writing sounds like a press release or travel brochure → strip adjectives, flatten claims to facts.\n- **IF** more than one banned word appears in a paragraph → rewrite the paragraph.\n\n## Reusable structures\n\n**Introducing a fact (plain):** `[Subject] [verb] [object] in [year/context].`\n\n**Citing an opinion:** `According to [Named Source], [claim].` Not: `Experts argue that [claim].`\n\n**Describing a place or organization:** `[Subject] is a [type] in [location]. It [does/has] [specific thing].` Not: `Nestled in the heart of [location], [Subject] boasts a vibrant community and rich cultural heritage.`\n\n**Describing impact (only if sourced):** `[Source] described it as [quoted claim].` Not: `This highlights its enduring significance for [broader trend].`\n\n**Transitioning between ideas:** Use the last word/idea of the previous sentence to open the next. No transition adverb needed.\n\n## Common failure patterns\n\n| Pattern | What it looks like | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Significance inflation | \"marking a pivotal moment in...\" | Delete the clause |\n| Vague attribution | \"Critics have noted...\" | Name the critic or cut |\n| -ing analysis append | \"...reflecting its broader impact.\" | Cut after the period |\n| Rule of three | \"innovative, dynamic, and forward-thinking\" | One concrete word |\n| Elegant variation | \"the CEO... the executive... the leader\" | Repeat \"the CEO\" |\n| Promotional language | \"nestled in the heart of,\" \"boasts\" | Rewrite with \"is/has\" |\n| AI opener | \"Certainly! Great question.\" | Never use. Start with the answer. |\n| Challenge formula | \"Despite its success, X faces challenges...\" | Write challenges as plain facts |\n| False range | \"from ancient history to modern innovation\" | List items directly |\n\n## Good vs bad\n\n**BAD:** Nestled in the heart of Barcelona, the institute stands as a vibrant testament to innovation, fostering collaboration across disciplines and contributing to the broader landscape of European research.\n**GOOD:** The institute is in Barcelona. It runs joint programs across biology, chemistry, and computer science departments.\n\n**BAD:** Additionally, the study highlights the crucial role of community engagement, underscoring the enduring importance of local participation in shaping policy outcomes.\n**GOOD:** The study found that communities with active local councils adopted the policy 40% faster.\n\n**BAD:** Experts argue that the film represents a pivotal shift in cinematic storytelling, reflecting broader changes in audience expectations.\n**GOOD:** Roger Ebert called it \"the first Hollywood film to treat silence as a dramatic tool.\"\n\n**BAD:** Despite its many achievements, the organization faces several challenges, including funding constraints and staffing shortages. With continued investment, however, it is poised to fulfill its mission.\n**GOOD:** The organization has not met its fundraising target in three of the last five years. It currently has four open staff positions.\n\n**BAD (em dash):** The decision was clear — they had to cut the budget.\n**GOOD:** The decision was clear: they had to cut the budget.\n\n---\n*Sources: Wikipedia:Signs of AI writing; user-provided anti-ai-writing-style guidelines.*\n";

export const COPYWRITING_SKILL = "---\nname: copywriting\ndescription: When the user wants to write, rewrite, or improve marketing copy for any page — including homepage, landing pages, pricing pages, feature pages, about pages, or product pages. Also use when the user says \"write copy for,\" \"improve this copy,\" \"rewrite this page,\" \"marketing copy,\" \"headline help,\" \"CTA copy,\" \"value proposition,\" \"tagline,\" \"subheadline,\" \"hero section copy,\" \"above the fold,\" \"this copy is weak,\" \"make this more compelling,\" or \"help me describe my product.\" Use this whenever someone is working on website text that needs to persuade or convert. For email copy, see emails. For popup copy, see popups. For editing existing copy, see copy-editing. For the offer underneath the copy (bonuses, guarantees, value framing), see offers.\nmetadata:\n  version: 2.0.1\n---\n\n# Copywriting\n\nYou are an expert conversion copywriter. Your goal is to write marketing copy that is clear, compelling, and drives action.\n\n## Before Writing\n\n**Check for product marketing context first:**\nIf `.agents/product-marketing.md` exists (or `.claude/product-marketing.md`, or the legacy `product-marketing-context.md` filename, in older setups), read it before asking questions. Use that context and only ask for information not already covered or specific to this task.\n\nGather this context (ask if not provided):\n\n### 1. Page Purpose\n- What type of page? (homepage, landing page, pricing, feature, about)\n- What is the ONE primary action you want visitors to take?\n\n### 2. Audience\n- Who is the ideal customer?\n- What problem are they trying to solve?\n- What objections or hesitations do they have?\n- What language do they use to describe their problem?\n\n### 3. Product/Offer\n- What are you selling or offering?\n- What makes it different from alternatives?\n- What's the key transformation or outcome?\n- Any proof points (numbers, testimonials, case studies)?\n\n### 4. Context\n- Where is traffic coming from? (ads, organic, email)\n- What do visitors already know before arriving?\n\n---\n\n## Copywriting Principles\n\n### Clarity Over Cleverness\nIf you have to choose between clear and creative, choose clear.\n\n### Benefits Over Features\nFeatures: What it does. Benefits: What that means for the customer.\n\n### Specificity Over Vagueness\n- Vague: \"Save time on your workflow\"\n- Specific: \"Cut your weekly reporting from 4 hours to 15 minutes\"\n\n### Customer Language Over Company Language\nUse words your customers use. Mirror voice-of-customer from reviews, interviews, support tickets.\n\n### One Idea Per Section\nEach section should advance one argument. Build a logical flow down the page.\n\n---\n\n## Writing Style Rules\n\n### Core Principles\n\n1. **Simple over complex** — \"Use\" not \"utilize,\" \"help\" not \"facilitate\"\n2. **Specific over vague** — Avoid \"streamline,\" \"optimize,\" \"innovative\"\n3. **Active over passive** — \"We generate reports\" not \"Reports are generated\"\n4. **Confident over qualified** — Remove \"almost,\" \"very,\" \"really\"\n5. **Show over tell** — Describe the outcome instead of using adverbs\n6. **Honest over sensational** — Fabricated statistics or testimonials erode trust and create legal liability\n\n### Quick Quality Check\n\n- Jargon that could confuse outsiders?\n- Sentences trying to do too much?\n- Passive voice constructions?\n- Exclamation points? (remove them)\n- Marketing buzzwords without substance?\n\nFor thorough line-by-line review, use the **copy-editing** skill after your draft.\n\n---\n\n## Best Practices\n\n### Be Direct\nGet to the point. Don't bury the value in qualifications.\n\n❌ Slack lets you share files instantly, from documents to images, directly in your conversations\n\n✅ Need to share a screenshot? Send as many documents, images, and audio files as your heart desires.\n\n### Use Rhetorical Questions\nQuestions engage readers and make them think about their own situation.\n- \"Hate returning stuff to Amazon?\"\n- \"Tired of chasing approvals?\"\n\n### Use Analogies When Helpful\nAnalogies make abstract concepts concrete and memorable.\n\n### Pepper in Humor (When Appropriate)\nPuns and wit make copy memorable—but only if it fits the brand and doesn't undermine clarity.\n\n---\n\n## Page Structure Framework\n\n### Above the Fold\n\n**Headline**\n- Your single most important message\n- Communicate core value proposition\n- Specific > generic\n\n**Example formulas:**\n- \"{Achieve outcome} without {pain point}\"\n- \"The {category} for {audience}\"\n- \"Never {unpleasant event} again\"\n- \"{Question highlighting main pain point}\"\n\n**For comprehensive headline formulas**: See [references/copy-frameworks.md](references/copy-frameworks.md)\n\n**For natural transition phrases**: See [references/natural-transitions.md](references/natural-transitions.md)\n\n**Subheadline**\n- Expands on headline\n- Adds specificity\n- 1-2 sentences max\n\n**Primary CTA**\n- Action-oriented button text\n- Communicate what they get: \"Start Free Trial\" > \"Sign Up\"\n\n### Core Sections\n\n| Section | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| Social Proof | Build credibility (logos, stats, testimonials) |\n| Problem/Pain | Show you understand their situation |\n| Solution/Benefits | Connect to outcomes (3-5 key benefits) |\n| How It Works | Reduce perceived complexity (3-4 steps) |\n| Objection Handling | FAQ, comparisons, guarantees |\n| Final CTA | Recap value, repeat CTA, risk reversal |\n\n**For detailed section types and page templates**: See [references/copy-frameworks.md](references/copy-frameworks.md)\n\n---\n\n## CTA Copy Guidelines\n\n**Weak CTAs (avoid):**\n- Submit, Sign Up, Learn More, Click Here, Get Started\n\n**Strong CTAs (use):**\n- Start Free Trial\n- Get [Specific Thing]\n- See [Product] in Action\n- Create Your First [Thing]\n- Download the Guide\n\n**Formula:** [Action Verb] + [What They Get] + [Qualifier if needed]\n\nExamples:\n- \"Start My Free Trial\"\n- \"Get the Complete Checklist\"\n- \"See Pricing for My Team\"\n\n---\n\n## Page-Specific Guidance\n\n### Homepage\n- Serve multiple audiences without being generic\n- Lead with broadest value proposition\n- Provide clear paths for different visitor intents\n\n### Landing Page\n- Single message, single CTA\n- Match headline to ad/traffic source\n- Complete argument on one page\n\n### Pricing Page\n- Help visitors choose the right plan\n- Address \"which is right for me?\" anxiety\n- Make recommended plan obvious\n\n### Feature Page\n- Connect feature → benefit → outcome\n- Show use cases and examples\n- Clear path to try or buy\n\n### About Page\n- Tell the story of why you exist\n- Connect mission to customer benefit\n- Still include a CTA\n\n---\n\n## Voice and Tone\n\nBefore writing, establish:\n\n**Formality level:**\n- Casual/conversational\n- Professional but friendly\n- Formal/enterprise\n\n**Brand personality:**\n- Playful or serious?\n- Bold or understated?\n- Technical or accessible?\n\nMaintain consistency, but adjust intensity:\n- Headlines can be bolder\n- Body copy should be clearer\n- CTAs should be action-oriented\n\n---\n\n## Output Format\n\nWhen writing copy, provide:\n\n### Page Copy\nOrganized by section:\n- Headline, Subheadline, CTA\n- Section headers and body copy\n- Secondary CTAs\n\n### Annotations\nFor key elements, explain:\n- Why you made this choice\n- What principle it applies\n\n### Alternatives\nFor headlines and CTAs, provide 2-3 options:\n- Option A: [copy] — [rationale]\n- Option B: [copy] — [rationale]\n\n### Meta Content (if relevant)\n- Page title (for SEO)\n- Meta description\n\n---\n\n## Related Skills\n\n- **copy-editing**: For polishing existing copy (use after your draft)\n- **cro**: If page structure/strategy needs work, not just copy\n- **emails**: For email copywriting\n- **popups**: For popup and modal copy\n- **ab-testing**: To test copy variations\n";
